A||||4||86_Fastback||Freeze plug
Z||000000||86_Fastback||06-25-99||05:48 AM||||Anyone replaced a freeze plug before?  What does it take to do one? Any special tools needed?<br>-Gregg
Z||000001||DJRice||06-25-99||06:41 AM||||I replaced a freeze plug on my 84 4 cyl....<br>I had to remove the exhaust manifold to do it.  I used one of those rubber expansion plugs to replace it and it is holding up well.  If you have a 6cyl, I feel for you becuase It would seem to me the only way to do it would be to remove the engine completely.  Doing the 4 cyl was an all day job.
Z||000002||86_Fastback||06-25-99||07:37 AM||||DJRice,<br>I do have the v-6.  Is the biggest problem getting at it?  
Z||000003||theogre||06-25-99||08:13 AM||||If you can get to it, you just knock it into the hole and pull it out sidways. If you are worried it might get away from you drill a hole in the plug, insert a screw with a string tied to it, then knock it into the block. just hit it near a side and it will pop it, dont try to hit it right on the edge or you'll score up the hole.<p>New freeze plugs just drive into the hole after you clean up any loose stuff with a small wire brush. (The tooth brush sized ones are great for this.) Give the hole a  Very THIN coat of RTV before you drive the new plug. The plug will collect the RTV in front of it and stop any fine scratches from leaking. Use a big socket or pipe the fills the dished side of the new plug, but not tightly. If your socket is tight in the dish it might get stuck when RTV sealer cure awhile before refilling the cooling system.<p>Using those rubber plugs is only safe in places that don't see allot of cold weather. Those things won't pop out should the block ever freeze. It sounds like allot to use a new plug, but it's only about 10 minutes to fix once you get access to it.
Z||000004||DJRice||06-28-99||08:10 AM||||Right On Ogre, I live in Central Florida.  If it freezes bad enough to blow freeze plugs out of an engine here, Chances are that Florida has completely ground to a halt!
